      <h2>
        One workspace for running the agency.
      </h2>

      <p>
        Retention Engine is a Notion operating system for managing clients,
        projects, content production, profitability, reporting and delivery —
        organised so the day-to-day of agency work lives in one place.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="case-section-label">01 / Context</div>

      <h2>Context</h2>

      <p>
        Agency work often gets fragmented across project boards, client
        databases, content calendars, reporting documents and financial
        trackers. Retention Engine was designed as a single operating layer
        that connects these moving parts into one workspace.
      </p>

      <p>
        The system brings client relationships, project delivery, content
        production, performance tracking and financial visibility into a
        connected operational structure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="case-section-label">02 / The problem</div>

      <h2>The problem</h2>

      <p>
        As an agency grows, operational information tends to spread across
        disconnected tools and views. Projects live separately from client
        context, content production from delivery, and financial tracking
        from day-to-day execution.
      </p>

      <p>
        The result is more manual coordination, less visibility and a higher
        risk of losing important context between teams, projects and clients.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="case-section-label">03 / Objective</div>

      <h2>Objective</h2>

      <p>
        The objective was to create one operating system that could give an
        agency a clear view of what is happening, who owns it, which client
        or project it belongs to, and how delivery connects to business
        performance.
      </p>

      <p>
        The system needed to support both daily execution and higher-level
        operational visibility without turning the workspace into a
        collection of disconnected databases.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="case-section-label">04 / Research / Approach</div>

      <h2>Research / Approach</h2>

      <p>
        I mapped the recurring operational layers of agency work and
        organized them into connected workspaces rather than isolated
        trackers.
      </p>

      <p>
        The system was structured around five core areas: pipeline and
        projects, client management, content production, strategy and
        analytics, with database relationships connecting the operational
        layer underneath.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each workspace was designed around a specific decision or workflow
        rather than simply storing information.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="case-section-label">05 / Key findings</div>

      <h2>Key findings</h2>

      <p>
        The system revealed that the main operational challenge is not the
        absence of information, but the separation of information.
      </p>

      <p>
        Client context influences projects. Projects generate production
        tasks. Production feeds reporting. Reporting informs strategy.
        Financial tracking adds the business layer needed to understand
        whether delivery is sustainable.
      </p>

      <p>
        Connecting these relationships creates a more useful operational
        picture than maintaining separate trackers for each function.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <div class="case-section-label">06 / Strategic thinking</div>

      <h2>Strategic thinking</h2>

      <p>
        The core design principle was to treat the agency as one connected
        system rather than a collection of workflows.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead of creating a separate tool for every operational problem,
        Retention Engine uses connected databases and purpose-built views to
        surface the same underlying information from different perspectives.
      </p>

      <p>
        This makes the system adaptable: the same project, client or task can
        appear where it is operationally relevant without duplicating the
        underlying data.
      </p>
    </section>

